(Aside: That's not what people mean by the protection racket, and it's
not what I was objecting to above where pacifist challenge was
specifically mentioned.  But if you're limiting levels much beyond
normal growth rates, it's probably still not the right tradeoff--of
course levelling up without exploring the dungeon isn't the right
tradeoff either).

> There's a lot of
> money in the top 4 levels of the Dungeon and the Mines.

Yes.  Enough that you'll probably get 2-3 buys of protection without
artificially limiting levels at all.  That's key to keep in mind.  The
racket isn't getting you 10 more points of immediate AC than you'd get
without it, it's getting you maybe 5-6 more on average.  And you'll be
coming back to get those other 5-6 points soon enough.

It's also important to realize that the benefit of AC isn't linear.
Getting from 10 to 0 AC is _far_ more helpful than getting from 0 to
-10 AC.  Indeed, getting from 10 to 0 AC is _far_ more helpful than
getting from -10 to -50 AC.

Points of AC down to AC 0 stop things from hitting you as often.
Beyond that, they only decrease how much damage things do on average,
but that decrease is random (from as little as 1hp off no matter how
low your AC up to the max).  So every additional point of AC below 0 is
worth less and less.

The same can be said of other things, of course; getting your HP from
10 to 40 is going to help your survivability a lot more than going from
40 to 70, and getting them from 500 to 1000 is borderline worthless.

So improving them all at the same rate has more value than improving
one twice as much and the other not at all.

> Getting back to your serial ascenders, what's the early game strategy then?
> What do they do to get a leg up on the Dungeon to get to mid and late game?

You try to balance many goals, and not focus on one to the exclusion of
others.

Make a list of key early-to-mid-game goals.  Mine is something like:
Getting your AC down is a key goal.
Getting your HP up is a key goal.
Getting your PW up is a key goal for spellcasters.
Getting poison resistance is a key goal.
Getting sleep resistance is a key goal.
Getting reflection and magic resistance are key goals.
Improving your combat/damage dealing abilities (missile and melee, and
spell failure)
Getting speed/jumping is a key goal.
Getting some "outs" (/tele, /digging, ?tele, /fire, /lightning, etc) is
a key goal
Getting MC3 is a key goal
Maxing your luck is a key goal

Obviously, some of these are easier than others.  Getting magic
resistance before the castle/Quest is only guaranteed for wizards,
otherwise it needs a lucky random item/bones file or a wish, or a gray
dragon in Ludios.  On the other hand, it's not usually as critical on
DL 1-7 as getting poison resistance, improving AC, or improving hp.

So what does levelling up gain you?
* More HP
* More PW
* Better chance to hit in combat
* More skill slots
* Lower spell failure

At least a couple of those (spell failure, PW) are only useful to
casters, which maybe indicates that spellcasters should be biased even
more toward levelling up than others.  Some of them have pretty
strongly quantized effects: getting dagger to skilled or expert
increases your fire rate, which helps missile effectiveness
dramatically.

What does levelling up cost you?
* Tougher monsters generated
* Pricier protection

The first of these is an odditiy, since there are plateaus where it's
particularly bad--going from XL1/DL1 monsters to XL2/DL2 monsters isn't
bad, but when elves start getting generated, all of a sudden your "get
out of combat free" card stops working consistently.  Urak hai and
others carry poison arrows, which are also non-E respecting.  But even
things like soldier ants can be managed somewhat safely with judicious
use of E--especially if you're a spellcaster who can safely blast them
at range without having to go pick up missiles, or if you have enough
missiles (e.g. a ranger) to take them down before stepping off your
safe haven.

So what's my course of action?

Well, initial improvements to AC and HP are worth a _ton_ in terms of
survivability.  I tend to view AC -5/HP 50 as early goals.  Until I'm
at AC -5, I will drag all kinds of armor through the dungeon to
BUC-test it and see if it's enchanted.  After that I'll get a bit
lazier about it (which I rationalize as "the payoff of a point of AC
isn't worth the danger of using lots of turns on non-exploratory
actions", but lazy is probably more accurate).  I'll level up freely,
but I want to keep moving quickly so that I'm exploring more of the
dungeon and finding armor and useful items.  The longer I hang around
on a level, the more I'm just levelling up without helping the other
goals.

Minetown and sokoban are good ways to reach some of those early goals:
soko has 50/50 reflection, but also has lots of wands that could be
good outs.  Minetown has most importantly a guaranteed altar to BUC all
the armor you find.  It also has potentially good items, guards who
(after being pet fodder) will drop gloves, small shields, etc, a
reasonable chance of a magic lamp, protection to be bought, etc.

I go back and forth over which is more valuable, but normally these
days I'll head for Minetown first.

Normally I reach Minetown around XL6-8 or so, with enough gold to buy
at least 1 level of protection (more if I credit clone or whatever,
though I find the danger of spending time in one spot isn't worth the
benefit of just 1 AC).  By this point, unless you're incredibly
unlucky, you'll have:

Iron shoes
Dwarvish helm
Dwarvish shield
Dwarvish cloak

That with, say, 4 points of protection gets you to AC0 and some MC.  It
doesn't take much of a lucky find (say, dwarvish mithril, or finding
one of your pieces of armor at +2 plus some studded leather) to have
your AC down to a fairly comfortable AC -5 or lower.

If you're careful about BUC testing, you'll get often get enchanted an
enchanted piece of armor or two.  That with your protection, the
helm/shield/shoes, gloves from a pet-food guard, an elven cloak and
some mithril can get you in the -10 range without ungodly luck.
Wishing up DSM or other "top 10%" luck can have you even better off.

And, of course, you'll be returning for protection whenever you get the
gold for another point.  By DL 15-20 or so you'll be at full
protection.

At this time you also explore Minetown fully for useful items (lamps!)
and so forth.  If Minetown is above DL7,  you can also explore the
levels below it down to (and including) DL7 at this time, assuming you
have the outs you need to escape a bad pack of monsters or whatver.
I'll explore if I still lack mithril, need more daggers, haven't found
a pick/shoes/helm, etc--if I'm pretty sure there's a high likelihood of
it giving me a good return, and I feel pretty safe doing it (say, have
a /fire or something else to get out of trouble).

Hopefully along the way you've had some luck finding poison resistance.
 But often you hadn't--having neither PR nor MC3 (which at least gives
you 98% protection from "the poison was deadly!" from bees, ants, etc)
is fairly rare, and almost always you'll have at least MC2 by now.

This is usually where you need to start getting inventive.  The next
goal is generally to get the goodies sokoban (hoping for reflection/BoH
and useful rings/wands, and maybe enough gold for more protection).
But lack of PR can potentially interfere with getting there (or staying
there).  Getting a unicorn horn helps a lot since you can eat poisonous
corpses with impunity (and the unicorn's corpse could grant PR itself).
 Some of the top players will ignore the Str loss as unimportant
compared to PR--mrivan doesn't hesitate to eat any potential
PR-granting corpse, then make do with decreased strength until he
manages to find a unihorn.  A tinning kit may be handy.  Any food shops
with ice chests that you've found (or random ice chests in the dungeon)
are worth checking for PR-granting corpses.  Every tin in the dungeon
is potential PR (always buy shop tins!).  A /insight can help you ID
rings/amulets of poison resistance.

Also at around this point elves can start showing up.  They don't
respect E, so you should run for choke points (corridors, whatever) or
the stairs when they appear.  Whether to take them on depends on your
specifics, but they are usually not _that_ tough and they can drop some
nice goodies (including musical instruments and corpses of sleep
resistance).  Finding the downstairs ASAP on each level gives you 2
panic routes out (in case a baddie gets spawned between you and the up
stairs).

Eventually you should reach sokoban.  After clearing it, you're at
another decision point.

If you have MR, clearing Mines' end for the luckstone is probably the
right course of action.  After that,  you can max your luck out (if
you've found an aligned altar or one you can convert) and proceed to
the quest or castle as you see fit.

If you lack MR, you are probably looking at a controlled dive to the
castle.  The goal here is to find a musical instrument, then make your
way carefully but speedily down to the castle (have a wand of digging
to bypass Titans at medusa), use Elbereth and any offense you can
muster to deal with potential purple L at the castle, and then use the
drawbridge to crush the castle denizens and claim the castle wand.
Avoid walking on squares you don't need to walk on, as levelports and
polytraps are a nuisance.

Before a castle dive, you might consider trying to #dip for a wish in
every fountain starting at DL1 and down to DL8 or so (odds of a wish
get pretty slim below that).  Make sure you have no gold out, dip an
item that you want uncursed if possible (and not one that you want
cursed), and have a way to deal with all the possible negative effects
(Juiblex and others can be summoned by water demons).

Once you've cleared the Castle, you should be good to ascend.  Only
extremely unlikely combinations of RNG or carelessness should get to
you now (unless you're trying odd conducts)--well, that's not true.
Once you've been past the Castle a few times and learned the new perils
there, then it should be pretty safe.  A first-time post-Castle game
still has some unlearned surprises, but if you play carefully you
should have enough options at your disposal to escape most situations.

